==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|expedient|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|expediens|lang=la}} (stem ''expedient-),'' present participle of {{m|la|expedire||to bring forward, to dispatch, to [[expedite]]; impers. to be profitable, serviceable, advantageous, expedient}}, from {{m|la|ex||out}} + {{m|la|pes}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɪkˈspiːdiənt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-expedient.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[simple|Simple]], [[easy]], or [[quick]]; [[convenient]].
#: ''Most people, faced with a [[decision]], will choose the most '''expedient''' option.''
#* Bible, John xvi. 7
#*: It is '''expedient''' for you that I go away.
#* Whately
#*: Nothing but the right can ever be '''expedient''', since that can never be true expediency which would sacrifice a greater good to a less.
# Governed by [[self-interest]], often short-term self-interest.
#* '''1861''', John Stuart Mill, ''[[s:Utilitarianism|Utilitarianism]]''
#*: But the '''Expedient''', in the sense in which it is opposed to the Right, generally means that which is '''expedient''' for the particular interest of the agent himself; as when a minister sacrifices the interests of his country to keep himself in place.
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} Quick; rapid; [[expeditious]].
#* Shakespeare
#*: His marches are '''expedient''' to this town.

